kipedia:Epithelium\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:/10.1016/B978-0-12-410424-2.00003-2\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.biologyonline.com/dictionary/stratified-epithelium\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000079 \"An epithelial cell, organized into multiple layers, with only the basal layer being in contact with the basement membrane.\")\n\n  Line 4550: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000079 \"stratified epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 4569: # Class: obo:CL_0000082 (epithelial cell of lung)\n\n  Line 4571: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000082 \"An epithelial cell of the lung.\")\n\n  Line 4573: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000082 \"lung epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 4574: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000082 \"epithelial cell of lung\")\n\n  Line 4577: # Class: obo:CL_0000083 (epithelial cell of pancreas)\n\n  Line 4579: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000083 \"An epithelial cell of the pancreas.\")\n\n  Line 4581: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000083 \"pancreas epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 4582: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000083 \"pancreatic epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 4583: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000083 \"epithelial cell of pancreas\")\n\n  Line 4751: # Class: obo:CL_0000098 (sensory epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 4753: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0721662544\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000098 \"A specialized epithelial cell involved in sensory perception. Restricted to special sense organs of the olfactory, gustatory, and vestibulocochlear receptor systems; contain sensory cells surrounded by supportive, non-receptive cells.\")\n\n  Line 4754: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym obo:CL_0000098 \"neuroepithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 4756: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000098 \"The term \\\"neuroepithelial cell\\\" is used to describe both this cell type and neurecto-epithelial cell (CL:0000710).\")\n\n  Line 4757: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000098 \"sensory epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 4911: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000115 \"From FMA: 9.07.2001: Endothelial cell has always been classified as a kind of epithelial cell, specifically a squamous cell but that is not true. First, endothelial cell can either be squamous or cuboidal (e.g. high-endothelial cell) and secondly, it has different embryological derivation (mesodermal) than a true epithelial cell (ectodermal and endodermal). The basis for present classification is the fact that it comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures (location-based) but a better structural basis for the differentia is the cytoskeleton of the cell. Endothelial cell has vimentin filaments while an epithelial cell has keratin filaments. [Onard].\")\n\n  Line 5237: # Class: obo:CL_0000146 (simple columnar epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 5239: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000146 \"simple columnar epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 5269: # Class: obo:CL_0000150 (glandular secretory epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 5271: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ORCID:0000-0002-7073-9172\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000150 \"An epithelial cell, located in a gland, that is specialised for the synthesis and secretion of specific biomolecules, such as hormones, or mucous.\")\n\n  Line 5274: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000150 \"glandular epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 5275: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000150 \"glandular secretory epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 5310: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21907708\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30571046\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Gastric_chief_cell\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000155 \"An epithelial cell of the stomach that is part of the fundic gastric gland. This cell is characterized by a basally located nucleus, abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum, and large apical secretory granules. It produces and secretes pepsinogen, the inactive precursor of the digestive enzyme pepsin.\")\n\n  Line 5356: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D020397\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goblet_cell\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000160 \"A specialized, columnar, mucus secreting epithelial cell shaped like a flask or goblet. A narrow basal end contains the nucleus while the apical end is swollen by the accumulation of mucus laden secretory granules.  Short microvilli project from the apical plasma membrane.\")\n\n  Line 5377: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31613538\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31670611\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000162 \"An epithelial cell of the stomach that is part of the fundic gastric gland. This cell is characterized by its pyramidal shape, abundant mitochondria, and a complex network of secretory canaliculi lined with microvilli. It secretes hydrochloric acid into the stomach lumen and produces intrinsic factor, essential for vitamin B12 absorption.\")\n\n  Line 5580: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0412046911\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D022781\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19717280\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hepatocyte\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000182 \"The main structural component of the liver. They are specialized epithelial cells that are organized into interconnected plates called lobules. Majority of cell population of liver, polygonal in shape, arranged in plates or trabeculae between sinusoids; may have single nucleus or binucleated.\")\n\n  Line 5605: # Class: obo:CL_0000185 (myoepithelial cell)\n\n  Line 5611: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000185 \"basket epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 5613: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000185 \"myoepithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 5837: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"JB:jb\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000213 \"OBSOLETE. A cell within an epithelial cell sheet whose main function is to act as an internal or external covering for a tissue or an organism.\")\n\n  Line 6071: # Class: obo:CL_0000237 (keratinizing barrier epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 6073: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000237 \"keratinizing barrier epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6078: # Class: obo:CL_0000238 (non keratinizing barrier epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 6080: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000238 \"non keratinizing barrier epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6083: # Class: obo:CL_0000239 (brush border epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 6085: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000239 \"An epithelial cell characterized by the presence of a brush border on its apical surface, which increases the surface area for absorption.\")\n\n  Line 6088: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000239 \"All brush border cells are columnar-cuboidal.  The formal logical definition of this term asserts this. Brush border epithelial cells are predominantly found lining the small intestine and the proximal tubules of the kidneys.\")\n\n  Line 6089: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000239 \"brush border epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6093: # Class: obo:CL_0000240 (stratified squamous epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 6095: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30422572\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Epithelium\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Stratified_squamous_epithelium\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000240 \"A stratified epithelial cell that is part of squamous epithelium, characterized by multiple layers of cells. The basal layer is directly attached to the basement membrane and the apical layer consists of flattened squamous cells. This provides a protective barrier, commonly found in areas subject to abrasion, such as the skin, oral cavity, and esophagus.\")\n\n  Line 6096: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000240 \"stratified squamous epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6100: # Class: obo:CL_0000241 (stratified cuboidal epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 6102: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30422572\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Stratified_cuboidal_epithelium\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000241 \"A stratified epithelial cell that is part of cuboidal epithelium, characterized by multiple layers of cuboidal cells forming the apical layer. This provides a protective lining for ducts in large glands, such as sweat glands.\")\n\n  Line 6103: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000241 \"stratified cuboidal epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6109: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D018862\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19834574\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2274847\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24862916\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30415995\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Merkel_cell\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000242 \"A specialized epithelial cell located in the skin epidermis and certain mucosal epithelia. It functions as a mechanoreceptor for light touch by forming synapse-like contacts with somatosensory afferent nerve endings, contributing to slowly adapting type I (SAI) tactile responses. Characterized by dense-core neuroendocrine granules, the Merkel cell exhibits both sensory and neuroendocrine properties, including regulated neurotransmitter release via SNARE complex-dependent mechanisms. Its development in mice depends on the transcription factor Atoh1.\")\n\n  Line 6127: # Class: obo:CL_0000244 (transitional epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 6131: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000244 \"transitional epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6555: # Class: obo:CL_0000307 (tracheal epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 6557: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000307 \"An epithelial cell found in the trachea.\")\n\n  Line 6560: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000307 \"This class is for the vertebrate tracheal structure. For the analagous insect cell type, see 'respiratory tube epithelial cell'\")\n\n  Line 6561: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000307 \"tracheal epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6589: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15749908\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19256306\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19727116\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29713660\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37873034\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000312 \"An epithelial cell of stratified squamous tissues, including skin, oral mucosa (Hiroshima et al., 2011), and esophagus (Whelan et al., 2018), that produces keratin proteins and secretes antimicrobial peptides to form a resilient barrier against environmental damage, dehydration, pathogens, and microbial invasion. It undergoes successive stages of differentiation marked by changes in keratin expression, supporting tissue integrity, wound repair, and contributing to immune defense.\")\n\n  Line 6630: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D012629\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19944183\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000317 \"An epithelial cell that is part of a sebaceous gland. This cell produces and secretes sebum, an oily, lipid-rich substance, through holocrine secretion where the entire cell ruptures to release its contents.\")\n\n  Line 6668: # Class: obo:CL_0000322 (pulmonary alveolar epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 6670: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20054144\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29463737\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000322 \"An epithelial cell that lines the peripheral gas exchange region of the lungs of air-breathing vertebrates.\")\n\n  Line 6677: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000322 \"alveolar epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6678: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000322 \"alveolus of lung epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6679: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000322 \"epithelial cell of alveolus of lung\")\n\n  Line 6682: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000322 \"pulmonary alveolar epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 6969: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Flybase:dsj\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MA:ma\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000362 \"An epithelial cell of the integument (the outer layer of an organism).\")\n\n  Line 6975: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"FMA:62411\") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:CL_0000362 \"epithelial cell of skin\")\n\n  Line 7317: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.wormatlas.org/hermaphrodite/hypodermis/mainframe.htm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000411 \"An epithelial cell of the hypodermis of Caenorhabditis.\")\n\n  Line 7368: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.wormatlas.org/ver1/handbook/hypodermis/hypsupportother.htm#arcadecells\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000418 \"An epithelial cell found in C. elegans that firmly hold the outer body wall and the lips to the inner cylinder of the pharynx in a manner that keeps these organs from breaking apart, while still giving each organ freedom of movement during feeding.\")\n\n  Line 7381: # Class: obo:CL_0000420 (syncytial epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 7383: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000420 \"An epithelial cell that forms a syncytium, which is a multinucleated cell resulting from the fusion of multiple cells.\")\n\n  Line 7385: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000420 \"syncytial epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 7444: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1007/s00412-016-0595-4\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000429 \"A columnar epithelial cell that is part of an insect imaginal disc.\")\n\n  Line 7445: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000429 \"This term does not encompass all the cell types that may be found in an epithelial disc (such as the peripodial or adepithelial cells), only the cells that form the disc epithelium proper.\")\n\n  Line 7575: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"FMA:69078\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000446 \"An epithelial cell of the parathyroid gland that is arranged in wide, irregular interconnecting columns; responsible for the synthesis and secretion of parathyroid hormone.\")\n\n  Line 7716: # Class: obo:CL_0000462 (adepithelial cell)\n\n  Line 7719: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000462 \"adepithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 7775: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000469 \"A neural progenitor cell that is the daughter of a neuroblast (sensu arthopoda).  The progeny of ganglion mother cells develop into neurons, glia and (occasionally) epithelial cells.\")\n\n  Line 7835: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000477 \"A somatic epithelial cell of the insect egg chamber.\")\n\n  Line 8007: # Class: obo:CL_0000500 (follicular epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 8010: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000500 \"follicular epithelial cell\")\n\n\n(Results truncated: showing 100 of 836 matches (736 hidden). Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)","metadata":{"matches":836,"truncated":true},"title":"epithelial cell|columnar epithelial|cuboidal epithelial|squamous epithelial","time":{"start":1778975690401,"end":1778975690459}},"id":"prt_e3336ce94001ZYC2d1rtPIRDWM","sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","messageID":"msg_e3336b56700155521eEs5RSiaX"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1778975690506,"sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","part":{"id":"prt_e3336cf07001wbdl5D7kYtJMTB","reason":"other","snapshot":"62cd9968dabe673b6c78fc2dea1c6309202b8205","messageID":"msg_e3336b56700155521eEs5RSiaX","sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":0,"input":0,"output":0,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":0}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1778975690617,"sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","part":{"id":"prt_e3336cf760013KsN7jaC7A1BjL","messageID":"msg_e3336cf2d001OWGZDSlnfX9cg6","sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","snapshot":"62cd9968dabe673b6c78fc2dea1c6309202b8205","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778975694820,"sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ft5nVgyReJVCnzcJEqoT3ywL","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/docs/relations_guide.md","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/docs/relations_guide.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Cell Ontology (CL) relations guide.\n2: \n3: \n4: ## Intro \n5: The aim of this document is to provide an accessible guide to how to use relations to record the properties that define cell types including location, lineage, function, morphology and marker genes. The term 'relations' here refers principally to OWL object properties, but also includes annoation properties used as shortcuts for more expressive logical axioms that can be programatically generated from them.\n6: \n7: Relations in this guide are grouped by general use case (e.g. recording location) and each is illustrated by an example e.g.-\n8: \n9: -   melanocyte subClassOf ‘has part’ some melanosome.\n10: \n11: This should be read as ‘**all** melanocytes have some type of melanosome as a part’ as should all axioms of this form. The examples should all be correct, but may not reflect the full complexity of axioms in the ontology. Where no example is currently present in CL, examples are taken from the Drosophila Anatomy Ontology, which follows the same schema.\n12: \n13: ## Recording location\n14: \n15: Location of cell types is recorded by relating a cell type to a term in an anatomical ontology. For the Cell Ontology this means a term from Uberon. \n16: \n17: ###  [**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050) \n18: \n19: Use part\\_of for cases where the location is a material anatomical structure (rather than a space, such as a sinus) and all of the cell is within the anatomical structure.\n20: \n21: ‘[epithelial cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000066)\n22: subClassOf [**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050)\n23: *some* [epithelium](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000483)\n24: \n25: ‘part of’ is transitive, which means that it applies across chains of relationships. For example,\n26: \n27: ‘ileal goblet cell’ part\\_of some ileum\n28: \n29: ilium ‘part of’ some ‘small intestine’\n30: \n31: ‘small intestine’ ‘part of’ some intestine\n32: \n33: =>\n34: \n35: ’ileal goblet cell’ ‘part of’ some ‘small intestine’\n36: &\n37: ‘ileal goblet cell’ ‘part of’ some intestine\n38: \n39: ### located\\_in\n40: \n41: To record the location of a cell in an anatomical space (e.g., a sinus),\n42: [**'located in'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001025) is used.\n43: \n44: For example:\n45: \n46: ‘[lymph node marginal reticular cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0009103)’ subClassOf [**'located in'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001025) *some* ['subcapsular sinus of lymph node'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0005463)\n47: \n48: ### overlaps\n49: \n50: [**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050) applies in cases where an entire cell is within an anatomical structure, but some cells have parts in multiple anatomical structures. For example, many neurons span multiple regions of the central nervous system. The general relation for this is [**overlaps**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002131) (has some part in).\n51: \n52: [**overlaps**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002131) is not currently used directly in the cell ontology (time of writing 05/2023), but more specific relationships exist for recording the location of neurons and their parts. These are described in the next section.\n53: \n54: ### Recording the location of neurons \n55: \n56: ### [<u>has soma location</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100)\n57: \n58: When neurobiologists talk about the location of vertebrate neurons, they are typically referring to soma location. The importance of soma location to identify is underscored by how commonly cell types are named, in part, by soma location. We therefore have a dedicated relation for recording this: [**'has soma location'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100).\n59: \n60: For example, [anterior horn motor neuron](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_2000048) has the following subclass axiom:\n61: \n62: [**'has soma location'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100) *some* ['ventral horn of spinal cord'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002257)\n63: \n64: **axiomatization of ‘has soma location’**\n65: \n66: -   *subPropertyOf*: overlaps \\# if X has\\_soma\\_location some Y, then X overlaps some Y)\n67: \n68: -   *domain*: neuron \\# X has\\_soma\\_location some Y => X is inferred to be a subClassOf neuron\n69: \n70: -   *property chain*: has\\_soma\\_location o part\\_of --> has\\_soma\\_location \\# If x has soma location y and y is part\\_of z, then x has\\_soma\\_location\\_z\n71: \n72: **Example of reasoning with the property chain:**\n73: \n74: 'cortical interneuron' equivalentTo 'interneuron' that has\\_soma\\_location some 'cerebral cortex' \n75: \n76: 'rosehip neuron' subClassOf interneuron  and has\\_soma\\_location some 'cortical layer 1' \n77: \n78: 'cortical layer 1'  subClassOf part\\_of some 'cerebral cortex\n79: \n80: => 'rosehip neuron' subClassOf 'cortical interneuron'\n81: \n82: ### sends synaptic output to region\n83: \n84: A relationship between a neuron and a region, where the neuron has a functionally relevant number of output synapses in that region.\n85: \n86: '[<u>adult basket subesophageal neuron</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00051856)' SubClassOf [<u>sends synaptic output to region</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0013003)\n87: *some* [<u>inferior posterior slope</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00045046)\n88: \n89: ### receives synaptic input in region\n90: \n91: A relationship between a neuron and a region, where the neuron has a functionally relevant number of input synapses:\n92: \n93: e.g. '[<u>adult basket subesophageal neuron</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00051856)' SubClassOf ‘[<u>receives synaptic input in region</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0013002)*’\n94: some ‘*<u>[superior posterior slope](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00045040)’</u>\n95: \n96: ### fasciculates\\_with\n97: \n98: Use this to record the tracts or nerves that a neuron’s projections fasciculate with.\n99: \n100: e.g. ‘Betz cell’ subClasssOf ‘fasciculates with’ some ‘corticospinal tract’.\n101: \n102: subPropertyOf: overlaps\n103: \n104: domain: neuron\n105: \n106: range: neuron projection bundle\n107: \n108: ## Recording synaptic connectivity (neurons)\n109: \n110: To record neuron-to-neuron or motor neuron-to-target muscle connectivity, consider the following object properties. These properties should be used when connectivity is key to the definition, for example, in cases where a motor neuron type is defined by the type of muscle fiber on which it synapses.\n111: \n112: ### [<u>synapsed to</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002120)\n113: \n114: For example, ['alpha motor neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008038) SubClassOf [**synapsed to**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002120) *some* ['extrafusal muscle fiber'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008046)\n115: \n116: ### [<u>synapsed by</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002103)\n117: \n118: -   This is the the inverse of [**synapsed to**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002120)\n119: \n120: For example, ['extrafusal muscle fiber'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008046) SubClassOf [**synapsed by**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002103) *some* ['alpha motor neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008038)\n121: \n122: ## Recording function\n123: \n124: Cellular function is recorded by linking GO biological process terms with the object properties [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) and ‘capable of part of’\n125: \n126: ###  [<u>'capable of'</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215)\n127: \n128: Use this relation where the cell is capable of carrying out the entirety of the process\n129: \n130: For example, ['hilus cell of ovary'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002095) has the following subclass:\n131: \n132: [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) *some* ['androgen secretion'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0035935)\n133: \n134: ### Recording neurotransmitter for neurons\n135: \n136: To record which neurotransmitter a neuron releases, use a [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) relation to link the neuron to a subclass of GO [neurotransmitter secretion](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0007269) that references a neurotransmitter type. This should be sufficient for autoclassification.\n137: \n138: e.g. ['medium spiny neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1001474) [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) some ['gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0061534)\n139: \n140: ### ‘capable of part of’\n141: \n142: Use this relationship where only part of the process occurs in the cell type.\n143: \n144: e.g.  'retinal bipolar neuron' 'capable of part of' some 'visual perception'\n145: \n146: ## Recording developmental lineage\n147: \n148: Developmental lineage is recorded between cell types with the object property [**develops from**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002202) (a transitive property), or in the case where there are no intermediates between the cells, [**'directly develops from'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002207) (a non-transitive subproperty of **develops_from**)\n149: \n150: For example, ['leukocyte'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000738) subClassOf [**develops from**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002202) *some* ['hematopoietic stem cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000037)\n151: \n152: ## Recording cell markers\n153: \n154: Only markers that are necessary to define a cell type should be recorded.\n155: \n156: ### cell surface (protein) markers\n157: \n158: The cell ontology has a set of terms for recording cell surface markers.\n159: \n160: The most commonly used relation for recording markers is [**'has plasma\n161: membrane part'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002104). This\n162: object property is used to record cell surface markers, especially in\n163: immune cells. There are also more specific properties, [**'has low\n164: plasma membrane amount'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0015016)\n165: and [**'has high plasma membrane\n166: amount'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0015015), that can be used\n167: at an editor's discretion. In each case, a term from the [PRotein\n168: Ontology (PRO)](https://github.com/PROconsortium/PRoteinOntology) or a\n169: protein complex term from the [Gene Ontology\n170: (GO)](https://github.com/geneontology/go-ontology) is used as the object\n171: of the relation.\n172: \n173: For example, ['alpha-beta T cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000789) has the following\n174: equivalence axiom:\n175: \n176: ['T cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000084) *and* [**'has\n177: plasma membrane part'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002104)\n178: *some* ['alpha-beta T cell receptor complex'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0042105)\n179: \n180: Absence of a marker can be recorded using\n181: [**lacks\\_plasma\\_membrane\\_part**](https://ontobee.org/ontology/CL?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2Fcl%23lacks_plasma_membrane_part)\n182:  \n183: \n184: Warning - this is used in place of the more accurate OWL expression \"NOT has_part some X*** in order to keep within the EL profile of OWL. It's use with a general class as a target can potentially lead to reasoning errors.  \n185: \n186: ### recording gene markers\n187: \n188: ### ‘expresses’\n189: \n190: Use this to link a cell type to a gene or gene product that defines it:\n191: \n192: For example:\n193: \n194: 'lamp5 GABAergic cortical interneuron' EquivalentTo:\n195: interneuron and ('has soma location' some 'cerebral cortex')\n196: and ('capable of' some 'gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission') \n197: and (expresses some 'lysosome-associated membrane glycoprotein 5')\n198: \n199: In FBbt, FlyBase Gene IDs are permitted here (using standard resolvable URL pattern).  In CL currenly only PRO IDs are permitted.  In PCL, a broader range of IDs have been used (depending on data sources used).\n200: \n201: ### Recording cell parts\n202: \n203: To record parts above the granularity of proteins and complexes, use a 'has part' relationship with an object from the Gene Ontology cellular_component branch.\n204: \n205: e.g. 'melanocyte' subClassOf 'has part' some 'melanosome'\n206: \n207: This GO term can be combined with a PATO quality term (e.g. for shape) where necessary, e.g.\n208: \n209: For example:\n210: \n211: 'mature basophil' subClassOf ('has part' some (nucleus and ('has characteristic' some lobed)))\n212: \n213: ## Recording general cellular characteristics\n214: \n215: The ontology [<u>PATO</u>](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/pato),\n216: has a rich set of terms that can be used to record the general\n217: characteristics of cells, such as their morphology. These are recorded\n218: using [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053).\n219: \n220: In choosing PATO terms, avoid those referring to some change in\n221: characteristic (e.g,.’ increased branchiness’). The following list of\n222: examples is not exhaustive:\n223: \n224: ### Recording Morphology\n225: \n226: PATO has a set of general morphology terms which may be applicable to\n227: cells.\n228: \n229: For example, [erythrocyte](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000765)\n230: subClassOf [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053)\n231: *some* [biconcave](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002039)\n232: \n233: PATO also has a set of terms for [<u>specific cell morphologies</u>](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/pato) (mostly\n234: neuronal), e.g.\n235: \n236: ‘Betz cell’ subClassOf ‘has characteristic’ some ‘standard pyramidal morphology’\n237: \n238: ### Recording nuclear number \n239: \n240: To record the number of nuclei in a cell, use a PATO subclass\n241: under the term ['nucleate quality'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001404) with the ['has\n242: characteristic'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) relation.\n243: \n244: ![image](https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/assets/112839/4b6aa141-c42f-4bff-9d9e-bdb71a17e882)\n245: \n246: For example, \n247: \n248: [platelet](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000233) subClassOf (['has_characteristic'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) *some* [**anucleate**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001405))\n249: \n250: Note - that PATO includes bridging axioms that infer part relationships\n251: based on these characteristics.\n252: \n253: e.g.\n254: \n255: cell and ('has characteristic' some multinucleate) SubClassOf 'has part'\n256: some nucleus\n257:                        \n258: ## Taxon constraints\n259: \n260: See\n261: <https://oboacademy.github.io/obook/explanation/taxon-constraints-explainer/>.\n262: \n\n(End of file - total 262 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Cell Ontology (CL) relations guide.\n\n\n## Intro \nThe aim of this document is to provide an accessible guide to how to use relations to record the properties that define cell types including location, lineage, function, morphology and marker genes. The term 'relations' here refers principally to OWL object properties, but also includes annoation properties used as shortcuts for more expressive logical axioms that can be programatically generated from them.\n\nRelations in this guide are grouped by general use case (e.g. recording location) and each is illustrated by an example e.g.-\n\n-   melanocyte subClassOf ‘has part’ some melanosome.\n\nThis should be read as ‘**all** melanocytes have some type of melanosome as a part’ as should all axioms of this form. The examples should all be correct, but may not reflect the full complexity of axioms in the ontology. Where no example is currently present in CL, examples are taken from the Drosophila Anatomy Ontology, which follows the same schema.\n\n## Recording location\n\nLocation of cell types is recorded by relating a cell type to a term in an anatomical ontology. For the Cell Ontology this means a term from Uberon. \n\n###  [**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050) \n\nUse part\\_of for cases where the location is a material anatomical structure (rather than a space, such as a sinus) and all of the cell is within the anatomical structure.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"docs/relations_guide.md","time":{"start":1778975694805,"end":1778975694817}},"id":"prt_e3336dfd1001NCg4qvCzWpdVR5","sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","messageID":"msg_e3336cf2d001OWGZDSlnfX9cg6"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778975694933,"sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_urL6qLBdpn6ETNI2uoke5a1q","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl","offset":4450,"limit":90},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n4450: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000065 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002207 obo:CL_0000681))\n4451: \n4452: # Class: obo:CL_0000066 (epithelial cell)\n4453: \n4454: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"FB:ma\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D004847\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000066 \"A cell that is usually found in a two-dimensional sheet with a free surface. The cell has a cytoskeleton that allows for tight cell to cell contact and for cell polarity where apical part is directed towards the lumen and the basal part to the basal lamina.\")\n4455: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"BTO:0000414\")\n4456: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"CALOHA:TS-2026\")\n4457: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"CARO:0000077\")\n4458: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"FMA:66768\")\n4459: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"WBbt:0003672\")\n4460: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000066 \"epitheliocyte\")\n4461: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000066 \"epithelial cell\")\n4462: AnnotationAssertion(foaf:depiction obo:CL_0000066 \"https://www.swissbiopics.org/api/image/Epithelial_cells.svg\"^^xsd:anyURI)\n4463: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000066 obo:CL_0000000)\n4464: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000066 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0000483))\n4465: DisjointClasses(obo:CL_0000066 obo:CL_0000738)\n4466: \n4467: # Class: obo:CL_0000067 (ciliated epithelial cell)\n4468: \n4469: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000067 \"An epithelial cell that has a cilia.\")\n4470: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000067 \"FMA:70605\")\n4471: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000067 \"ciliated epithelial cell\")\n4472: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0000067 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000066 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:GO_0005929)))\n4473: \n4474: # Class: obo:CL_0000068 (duct epithelial cell)\n4475: \n4476: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000068 \"An epithelial cell that is part of a duct.\")\n4477: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000068 \"duct epithelial cell\")\n4478: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0000068 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000066 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0000058)))\n4479: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000068 obo:CL_0000066)\n4480: \n4481: # Class: obo:CL_0000069 (branched duct epithelial cell)\n4482: \n4483: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000069 \"branched duct epithelial cell\")\n4484: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000069 obo:CL_0000068)\n4485: \n4486: # Class: obo:CL_0000070 (obsolete epithelial cell of gland)\n4487: \n4488: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000070 \"obsolete epithelial cell of gland\")\n4489: AnnotationAssertion(owl:deprecated obo:CL_0000070 \"true\"^^xsd:boolean)\n4490: \n4491: # Class: obo:CL_0000071 (blood vessel endothelial cell)\n4492: \n4493: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000071 \"An endothelial cell that lines the vasculature.\")\n4494: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000071 \"blood vessel endothelial cell\")\n4495: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0000071 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000115 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0001981)))\n4496: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000071 obo:CL_0002139)\n4497: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000071 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0004638))\n4498: \n4499: # Class: obo:CL_0000072 (non-branched duct epithelial cell)\n4500: \n4501: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000072 \"non-branched duct epithelial cell\")\n4502: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000072 obo:CL_0000068)\n4503: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000072 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002202 obo:CL_0000222))\n4504: \n4505: # Class: obo:CL_0000073 (obsolete barrier epithelial cell)\n4506: \n4507: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000073 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1149>)\n4508: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0100001 obo:CL_0000073 obo:CL_0000215)\n4509: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000073 \"obsolete barrier epithelial cell\")\n4510: AnnotationAssertion(owl:deprecated obo:CL_0000073 \"true\"^^xsd:boolean)\n4511: \n4512: # Class: obo:CL_0000074 (obsolete epithem cell)\n4513: \n4514: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12662305\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000074 \"Obsolete. Use PO:0000066 from Plant Ontology instead. A cell that constitutes the mesophyll of a hydathode and is located between the xylem endings and the epidermis. Proposed to be involved in the retrieval of solutes from the xylem sap.\")\n4515: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0100001 obo:CL_0000074 obo:PO_0000066)\n4516: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000074 \"obsolete epithem cell\")\n4517: AnnotationAssertion(owl:deprecated obo:CL_0000074 \"true\"^^xsd:boolean)\n4518: \n4519: # Class: obo:CL_0000075 (columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell)\n4520: \n4521: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO:0002065\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000075 \"A columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell is a cell usually found in a two dimensional sheet with a free surface. Columnar/cuboidal epithelial cells take on the shape of a column or cube.\")\n4522: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000075 \"columnar/cuboidal epithelial cell\")\n4523: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000075 obo:CL_0000066)\n4524: \n4525: # Class: obo:CL_0000076 (squamous epithelial cell)\n4526: \n4527: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000076 \"CALOHA:TS-1249\")\n4528: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000076 \"squamous epithelial cell\")\n4529: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000076 obo:CL_0000066)\n4530: \n4531: # Class: obo:CL_0000077 (mesothelial cell)\n4532: \n4533: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0721662544\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26106328\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000077 \"A flat, squamous-like epithelial cell of mesodermal origin. It forms the mesothelium, which lines the body's serous cavities including the pleural, peritoneal, and pericardial spaces. This cell plays a crucial role in synthesizing and secreting lubricants, such as glycosaminoglycans and surfactants, which minimize friction between adjacent tissues during movement.\")\n4534: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000077 \"FMA:66773\")\n4535: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000077 \"mesotheliocyte\")\n4536: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26106328\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000077 \"Mesothelial cells, which morphologically resemble squamous epithelial cells, express both epithelial markers (cytokeratins) and mesenchymal markers (vimentin), reflecting their mesodermal origin. They feature surface microvilli and apical-basal polarity. Under certain conditions, they can undergo mesothelial-to-mesenchymal transition. These cells also produce extracellular matrix proteins, maintain serosal homeostasis, and facilitate fluid transport.\")\n4537: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000077 \"mesothelial cell\")\n4538: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000077 obo:CL_0000076)\n4539: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000077 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0001136))\n\n(Showing lines 4450-4539 of 35602. Use offset=4540 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000065 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002207 obo:CL_0000681))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_0000066 (epithelial cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"FB:ma\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D004847\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000066 \"A cell that is usually found in a two-dimensional sheet with a free surface. The cell has a cytoskeleton that allows for tight cell to cell contact and for cell polarity where apical part is directed towards the lumen and the basal part to the basal lamina.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"BTO:0000414\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"CALOHA:TS-2026\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"CARO:0000077\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"FMA:66768\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000066 \"WBbt:0003672\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000066 \"epitheliocyte\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000066 \"epithelial cell\")\nAnnotationAssertion(foaf:depiction obo:CL_0000066 \"https://www.swissbiopics.org/api/image/Epithelial_cells.svg\"^^xsd:anyURI)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_0000066 obo:CL_0000000)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_0000066 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0000483))\nDisjointClasses(obo:CL_0000066 obo:CL_0000738)\n\n# Class: obo:CL_0000067 (ciliated epithelial cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000067 \"An epithelial cell that has a cilia.\")","truncated":true,"loaded":[]},"title":"src/ontology/cl-edit.owl","time":{"start":1778975694763,"end":1778975694930}},"id":"prt_e3336dfa1001NmErSITXF9MJ14","sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","messageID":"msg_e3336cf2d001OWGZDSlnfX9cg6"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778975694954,"sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_XGMDx5rPg4K4b5dFAychmOXc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl","offset":6080,"limit":35},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n6080: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000238 \"non keratinizing barrier epithelial cell\")\n6081: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000238 obo:CL_0000240)\n6082: \n6083: # Class: obo:CL_0000239 (brush border epithelial cell)\n6084: \n6085: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000239 \"An epithelial cell characterized by the presence of a brush border on its apical surface, which increases the surface area for absorption.\")\n6086: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000239 <https://doi.org/10.1152/ajpgi.00005.2011>)\n6087: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000239 \"PMID:25422372\")\n6088: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000239 \"All brush border cells are columnar-cuboidal.  The formal logical definition of this term asserts this. Brush border epithelial cells are predominantly found lining the small intestine and the proximal tubules of the kidneys.\")\n6089: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000239 \"brush border epithelial cell\")\n6090: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0000239 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000066 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:GO_0005903)))\n6091: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000239 obo:CL_0000075)\n6092: \n6093: # Class: obo:CL_0000240 (stratified squamous epithelial cell)\n6094: \n6095: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30422572\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Epithelium\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Stratified_squamous_epithelium\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000240 \"A stratified epithelial cell that is part of squamous epithelium, characterized by multiple layers of cells. The basal layer is directly attached to the basement membrane and the apical layer consists of flattened squamous cells. This provides a protective barrier, commonly found in areas subject to abrasion, such as the skin, oral cavity, and esophagus.\")\n6096: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000240 \"stratified squamous epithelial cell\")\n6097: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0000240 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000079 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0006915)))\n6098: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000240 obo:CL_0000076)\n6099: \n6100: # Class: obo:CL_0000241 (stratified cuboidal epithelial cell)\n6101: \n6102: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30422572\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Stratified_cuboidal_epithelium\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000241 \"A stratified epithelial cell that is part of cuboidal epithelium, characterized by multiple layers of cuboidal cells forming the apical layer. This provides a protective lining for ducts in large glands, such as sweat glands.\")\n6103: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000241 \"stratified cuboidal epithelial cell\")\n6104: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0000241 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000079 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0010077)))\n6105: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000241 obo:CL_0000075)\n6106: \n6107: # Class: obo:CL_0000242 (Merkel cell)\n6108: \n6109: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D018862\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19834574\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2274847\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24862916\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30415995\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Merkel_cell\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000242 \"A specialized epithelial cell located in the skin epidermis and certain mucosal epithelia. It functions as a mechanoreceptor for light touch by forming synapse-like contacts with somatosensory afferent nerve endings, contributing to slowly adapting type I (SAI) tactile responses. Characterized by dense-core neuroendocrine granules, the Merkel cell exhibits both sensory and neuroendocrine properties, including regulated neurotransmitter release via SNARE complex-dependent mechanisms. Its development in mice depends on the transcription factor Atoh1.\")\n6110: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000242 \"FMA:70548\")\n6111: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0000242 \"Merkel's cell\")\n6112: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24862916\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_0000242 \"MC\")\n6113: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000242 \"Merkel cell\")\n6114: SubClassOf(obo:CL_0000242 obo:CL_0000066)\n\n(Showing lines 6080-6114 of 35602. Use offset=6115 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000238 \"non keratinizing barrier epithelial cell\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_0000238 obo:CL_0000240)\n\n# Class: obo:CL_0000239 (brush border epithelial cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000239 \"An epithelial cell characterized by the presence of a brush border on its apical surface, which increases the surface area for absorption.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000239 <https://doi.org/10.1152/ajpgi.00005.2011>)\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:CL_0000239 \"PMID:25422372\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000239 \"All brush border cells are columnar-cuboidal.  The formal logical definition of this term asserts this. Brush border epithelial cells are predominantly found lining the small intestine and the proximal tubules of the kidneys.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000239 \"brush border epithelial cell\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0000239 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000066 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:GO_0005903)))\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_0000239 obo:CL_0000075)\n\n# Class: obo:CL_0000240 (stratified squamous epithelial cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30422572\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Epithelium\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Stratified_squamous_epithelium\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000240 \"A stratified epithelial cell that is part of squamous epithelium, characterized by multiple layers of cells. The basal layer is directly attached to the basement membrane and the apical layer consists of flattened squamous cells. This provides a protective barrier, commonly found in areas subject to abrasion, such as the skin, oral cavity, and esophagus.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000240 \"stratified squamous epithelial cell\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_0000240 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000079 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0006915)))\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_0000240 obo:CL_0000076)\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[]},"title":"src/ontology/cl-edit.owl","time":{"start":1778975694798,"end":1778975694953}},"id":"prt_e3336dfc7001SSna5Lb6CiPfn2","sessionID":"ses_1ccc97848ffe0WlvSbtl03avaE","messageID":"msg_e3336cf2d001OWGZDSlnfX9cg6"}}
